{% doc %}
  blocks/_tab.liquid

  One panel in a **Tabs** block, and the button that opens it.

  ## The button is built from a template, not from a string

  The button belongs in the rail and the panel belongs in the panel area, and
  `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` emits one stream into one container — so the
  button travels with the panel and `<tab-group>` in `assets/tabs.js` moves it.

  It travels as a `<template>` rather than as text on a `data-` attribute,
  because the button has an icon in it now. An SVG escaped into an attribute is
  markup that no longer looks like markup: nothing formats it, nothing checks
  it, and `snippets/icon.liquid` could not be used to produce it. A template is
  inert markup that the browser has already parsed, and `tabs.js` clones it.

  `data-tab-label` stays, and stays authoritative: it is what a panel with no
  template falls back to, and `blocks/menu-tab.liquid` still uses only that.

  ## The panel holds blocks, not a field

  Anything the theme has goes inside: a Section header over an Accordion is what
  the *FAQ: Tabs* preset builds, but a Text block, an Image, a Group or a set of
  Buttons are all the same block and none of them are named here. A tab that
  could only hold prose would be a tab that cannot hold a size table.

  Private — the leading underscore — because a panel outside a tab strip is a
  `role="tabpanel"` with no tab, which is markup a screen reader has to
  apologise for. It is addable inside **Tabs** and nowhere else.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings
  assign label = s.label | default: 'Tab'

  comment
    Icons only, no uploads. The button is a pill a few characters wide sitting
    in a rail, and an image in it is a second thing to size, crop and describe
    for a slot where the glyph set already covers what a tab name means.
  endcomment
  assign has_icon = false
  if s.icon_source == 'icon' and s.icon != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  capture icon_px
    render 'icon-size', size: s.icon_size, custom: s.icon_size_custom
  endcapture
  assign icon_size = icon_px | plus: 0

  comment
    The space between the blocks stacked in the panel — a Section header over an
    Accordion is the *FAQ: Tabs* preset, and until this existed the only way to
    separate the two was to reach into the accordion and give it a top padding
    it did not otherwise want.

    `gap_vertical` / `gap_vertical_mobile` rather than a name of this block's
    own, because that is the pair `blocks/card-content.liquid` and
    `blocks/image-card.liquid` already use for exactly this — one stack, one
    value per breakpoint — and a merchant who has spaced one of those has
    spaced this.

    The mobile value falls back to the desktop one rather than to a constant, so
    a panel saved before the setting existed keeps a single consistent stack
    instead of tightening at 749px for no reason the merchant chose.

    `| default: | plus: 0` and not a blank test: an unset range is nil in
    Shopify and Undefined elsewhere, and only one of those is blank — the
    difference that emits `--tab-content-gap:px` and takes the whole style
    attribute down with it.
  endcomment
  assign gap_block = s.gap_vertical | default: 24 | plus: 0
  assign gap_block_mobile = s.gap_vertical_mobile | default: gap_block | plus: 0

  comment
    ## The tab name's typography rides in the template

    The same three-part arrangement every text block in this theme uses — the
    named presets are `.block-text--*` classes in `assets/base.css` pointing at
    the tokens Theme settings › Typography writes, `block-typography` emits only
    the Custom preset and the colour, and `.tabs__tab-label` in
    `assets/header.css` reads the properties both of them set.

    It works here for the same reason the icon does: `tabs.js` clones the
    `<template>` into the button, and a clone carries its classes and its style
    attribute with it. Nothing has to be handed across on a `data-` attribute.

    Not `.block-text` itself — that class also sets `flex` and `inline-size`,
    and this span is a run of words inside a button, not a box. The typography
    is the part worth sharing.

    `default` is the default, so a tab saved before this existed emits no class
    that resolves and no declarations, and keeps the rail's own type.
  endcomment
  assign preset = s.preset | default: 'default'

  assign label_classes = 'tabs__tab-label block-text--' | append: preset
  if s.color == 'gradient'
    assign label_classes = label_classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  endif

  assign pill = s.pill
-%}

{%- capture label_style -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: s -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture pill_style -%}
  {%- if s.pill_background != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-pill-background: {{ s.pill_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.pill_color != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-pill-color: {{ s.pill_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture panel_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --tab-content-gap: {{ gap_block }}px;
  --tab-content-gap-mobile: {{ gap_block_mobile }}px;
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `tabindex="0"` because the panel is a scroll container of its own once the
  content is long enough, and a scrollable region has to be reachable from the
  keyboard. `id`, `aria-labelledby` and `hidden` are set by `tabs.js` as it
  builds the buttons, so they are not written twice here.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  class="tabs__panel"
  role="tabpanel"
  id="Tab-{{ block.id }}"
  data-tab-label="{{ label | escape }}"
  {%- comment -%}
    Presence, not a value: `tabs.js` tests the attribute with `!= null`, so an
    unchecked box has to emit nothing at all rather than `data-tab-selected="false"`.
    Only the first panel carrying it wins, which is what makes two merchants
    ticking two boxes a harmless setting rather than a broken strip.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% if s.selected %}data-tab-selected{% endif %}
  tabindex="0"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ panel_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <template data-tab-button>
    {%- if has_icon -%}
      <span class="tabs__tab-icon">{% render 'icon', icon: s.icon, size: icon_size %}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
    <span class="{{ label_classes }}" style="{{ label_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
      {{- label | escape -}}
    </span>
    {%- comment -%}
      The flag above the tab name — "Most Popular" on a plan chooser, "New" on a
      collection strip. It rides in the same template as the name because it
      belongs to the button, not to the panel: it is what the strip says about
      the option before the option is chosen.

      `aria-hidden` and not part of the accessible name. The name is the
      frequency, and a screen reader announcing "Most Popular Every 2 Month" as
      one string turns a marketing flag into part of the choice. Sighted
      customers read it as the decoration it is, and it stays available to them.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if pill != blank -%}
      <span class="tabs__tab-pill" style="{{ pill_style | strip_newlines | strip }}" aria-hidden="true">
        {{- pill | escape -}}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </template>

  {%- comment -%}
    The animation goes on a layer inside the panel, not on the panel itself.
    A panel already animates when the tab changes — `tab-panel-rise` in
    `assets/header.css`, which fires because `hidden` is `display: none` and a
    box that starts being rendered starts its animations. Two animations on one
    element is one of them winning per frame, so this one takes the layer under
    it and staggers the blocks, which is what **Stagger between items** offers.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <motion-effect
    class="tabs__panel-content"
    {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', block: s, children: true %}
  >
    {% content_for 'blocks' %}
  </motion-effect>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.tab.name",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "@theme" },
    { "type": "@app" }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "label",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.label.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.label.info",
      "default": "Tab"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "pill",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "pill_background",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill_background.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.pill != blank }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "pill_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill_color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.pill != blank }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "selected",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.selected.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.selected.info",
      "default": false
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.group.headers.layout" },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_vertical",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.gap_vertical.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.gap_vertical.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 60,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 24
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_vertical_mobile",
      "label": "t:blocks.image_card.settings.gap_vertical_mobile.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 60,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 16
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.subheading.headers.icon" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon_source",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.icon_source.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.none.label" },
        { "value": "icon", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "none"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "star", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "check", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "heart", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "tag", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "truck", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__5.label" },
        { "value": "refresh", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__6.label" },
        { "value": "lock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__7.label" },
        { "value": "clock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__8.label" },
        { "value": "location", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__9.label" },
        { "value": "mail", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__10.label" },
        { "value": "note", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__11.label" },
        { "value": "info", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__12.label" }
      ],
      "default": "note",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'icon' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon_size",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_size.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "extra_small", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "small", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "medium", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "large", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "extra_large", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__5.label" },
        { "value": "custom", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__6.label" }
      ],
      "default": "medium",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'icon' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "icon_size_custom",
